--- a/SPEC.md
+++ b/SPEC.md
@@ -1324,7 +1324,7 @@ host failure in one worker leaves the units the other workers hold running.

 - [RC-OWNERSHIP-PLAN-1] Experimental RC planning distinguishes physical owning edges from logical copy-on-write needs and unique resource drops. Immutable bytes and pure recursive boxes still carry lifetime obligations. Raw external resources have no RC header: their shapes contain no count edge, and their existing unique-drop and consumed-parameter obligations remain separate. Owned results are the canonical call convention, and only an explicit every-return physical certificate authorizes a borrowed result. Value versions, conditional ownership, caller storage and non-sharing scope holds are separate compiler facts; failure cleanup names precise lowering checkpoints. Canonical physical slots receive one initialization guard, and guarded local releases follow the real CFG, final reference liveness and pending operands. Selected RC metadata compiles against its versioned C ABI; GC remains the default runtime.

-- [RC-RUNTIME-CONTRACT-1] Native memory selection uses explicit versioned GC, RC and counted-RC ownership contracts; unknown versions are rejected, inherited RC cannot change legacy-sharing behavior, and an explicit RC request with legacy sharing is incompatible. Lowering reads one normalized process selection rather than independently parsing environment state. RC remains experimental with GC selected until its acceptance gate passes.
+- [RC-RUNTIME-CONTRACT-1] Native memory selection uses explicit versioned GC, RC and counted-RC ownership contracts; current RC uses ABI3, retired ABI1/ABI2 and unknown versions are rejected, inherited RC cannot change legacy-sharing behavior, and an explicit RC request with legacy sharing is incompatible. Lowering reads one normalized process selection rather than independently parsing environment state. RC remains experimental with GC selected until its acceptance gate passes.

 - [RC-UNIQUE-LOWER-1] Experimental RC mutations check physical owner counts through typed guarded slots. Failed detachment leaves the original owner intact and preserves its runtime status; borrowed inputs adopt only a fresh clone. Semantic COW loop hoisting remains disabled until physical temporary ownership is proved compatible.

